building on the three european council  meetings already devoted to the  migration crisis since april this year  EU leaders are expected to focus their  discussions on external borders this is  in fact the fourth summit in only six  months that will mainly focus on the  current migration and refugee crisis as  I see there will be three key objectives  of the summit first to take stock of the  decisions already taken to make sure  that they are in fact implemented to to  give directions at the highest level on  future policies and actions and here I  expect that they will most that leaders  will mostly focus on how to cooperate  with key countries of origin and transit  not least Turkey and Syria and third to  launch a debate on II use future  migration policy in the mid and  long-term strengthening the e use  external borders will include a call for  enlarging the Mandate of frontex and  developing a European border and coast  guard system in support of requesting  member states an increased role for  Frontex would also include the rights to  organize return operations and the  creation of a dedicated returner office  within Frontex  addressing the European Parliament at  the beginning of the month European  Council President Donald Tusk urged his  partners to fully measure the extent of  the current challenges we have to  finally say it loud and clear Europe  without its external borders equals  Europe without Schengen Europe without  its external borders will become a  breeding ground for fear in each and  every one of us and this will lead us  sooner than later to a political  catastrophe EU leaders are also expected  to call for effective follow-up to the  high-level conference on the Western  Balkans route and to achieve concrete  results at the upcoming valletta summit  with African partners on the eleventh  and twelfth of November
 

